eference among themselves and (save for certain obligations required to be preferred by French law) equally and rateably with any other existing or future deeply subordinated obligations outstanding from time to time, to the extent required by the Applicable Supervisory Regulations (and in particular the last paragraph of article 38(1) of the IRRD, as finally implemented under French law and taking into account any grandfathering regime thereunder) for so long as any such deeply subordinated obligations continue to constitute (or would constitute but for any applicable limitation on the amount of such capital) Tier 1 Own Funds of the Issuer and/or the Group under the then Applicable Supervisory Regulations; (ii) senior to all present and future Equity Securities; and (iii) junior to all present and future: (A) prêts participatifs granted to, and titres participatifs issued by, the Issuer; (B) ordinary subordinated obligations; (C) subordinated obligations preferred by mandatory and/or overriding provisions of law; (D) subordinated obligations of the Issuer that rank or are expressed to rank senior to deeply subordinated obligations (including senior subordinated obligations and 1 st ranking senior subordinated notes); and (E) Unsubordinated Obligations. If any judgment is rendered by any competent court declaring the judicial liquidation ( liquidation judiciaire) or, following an order of redressement judiciaire, the sale of the whole business ( cession totale de l’entreprise ) of the Issuer, or if the Issuer is liquidated for any reason, the rights of the Noteholders in respect of principal and interest will be subordinated to the payments of claims of other creditors of the Issuer including insurance companies and entities referred to in article R.322-132 of the French Code des assurances reinsured by the Issuer, holders of insurance policies issued by such entities and creditors with respect to obligations referred to in (iii) above , but will be paid in priority to any holders of, or creditors in respect of, obligations referred to in (ii) above. In the event of incomplete payment of creditors ranking senior to holders of the Notes (in the context of voluntary or judicial liquidation of the Issuer, bankruptcy proceedings or any other similar proceedings affecting the Issuer) the obligations of the Issuer in connection with the Notes and relative interest will be terminated. 3.2 Dynamic ranking To the extent and for so long as required or permitted by the Applicable Supervisory Regulations (and in particular the last paragraph of article 38(1) of the IRRD, as finally implemented under French law and taking into account any grandfathering regime t hereunder), and without prejudice to any implementation measure of the IRRD in France: 1. Should the Notes no longer be treated as Tier 1 Own Funds but be treated as tier 2 own funds regulatory capital (“Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 2 Own Funds”), and for so long as they constitute Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 2 Own Funds, they will cease to constitute deeply subordinated obligations of the Issuer, and will automatically constitute ordinary subordinated obligations of the Issuer without the need for any action from the Issuer and without consultation of the Noteholders. The ordinary subordinated obligations of the Issuer rank and shall at all times rank: (i) pari passu without any preference among themselves and (save for certain obligations required to be preferred by French law) equally and rateably with any other existing or future ordinary subordinated obligations of the Issuer; (ii) senior to all present and future: EMEA 149441201 56 (A) Equity Securities; (B) deeply subordinated obligations; (C) prêts participatifs granted to, and titres participatifs issued by, the Issuer; and (iii) junior to all present and future: (A) subordinated obligations preferred by mandatory and/or overriding provisions of law; (B) subordinated obligations of the Issuer that rank or are expressed to rank senior to ordinary subordinated obligations (including senior subordinated obligations and 1 st ranking senior subordinated notes); and (C) Unsubordinated Obligations. If any judgment is rendered by any competent court declaring the judicial liquidation ( liquidation judiciaire) or, following an order of redressement judiciaire, the sale of the whole business ( cession totale de l’entreprise ) of the Issuer, or if the Issuer is liquidated for any reason, the rights of the Noteholders in respect of principal and interest of the Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 2 Own Funds will be subordinated to the payments of claims of other creditors of the Issuer including insurance companies and entities referred to in article R.322 -132 of the French Code des assurances reinsured by the Issuer, holders of insurance policies issued by such entities and creditors with respect to obligations referred to in (iii) above, but will be paid in priority to any holders of, or creditors in respect of, obligations referred to in (ii) above. In the event of incomplete payment of creditors ranking senior to holders of the Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 2 Own Funds (in the context of voluntary or judicial liquidation of the Issuer, bankruptcy proceedings or any othe r similar proceedings affecting the Issuer) the obligations of the Issuer in connection with the Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 2 Own Funds and relative interest will be terminated. 2. Should the Notes no longer be treated as Tier 1 Own Funds or tier 2 own funds regulatory capital, but be treated, to the extent possible at that time, as tier 3 own funds regulatory capital (“ Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 3 Own Funds ”), and for so long as they constitute Notes Disqualified as Tier 1 Own Funds but Qualified as Tier 3 Own Funds, they will cease to